{"park":"lake_clark","fetched":"2026-09-07T03:25:43+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 69114"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/2758123854","name":"Mount Iliamna","kind":"peak","lon":-153.09152,"lat":60.03218,"ele_m":3053.0,"wikidata":"Q1950549","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Iliamna","tour":7,"summary":{"extract":"Iliamna Volcano, or Mount Iliamna, is a glacier-covered stratovolcano in the largely volcanic Aleutian Range in southwest Alaska. Located in the Chigmit Mountain subrange in Lake Clark National Park and Preserve, the 10,016-foot (3,053 m) volcano lies approximately 134 miles (215 km) southwest of Anchorage on the west side of lower Cook Inlet. It is the 25th most prominent peak in the United States.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Iliamna","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/2747492278","name":"Mount Redoubt","kind":"peak","lon":-152.74509,"lat":60.4857,"ele_m":3108.0,"wikidata":"Q840735","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Redoubt","tour":6,"summary":{"extract":"Redoubt Volcano, or Mount Redoubt, is an active stratovolcano in the largely volcanic Aleutian Range of the U.S. state of Alaska. Located at the head of the Chigmit Mountains subrange in Lake Clark National Park and Preserve, the mountain is just west of Cook Inlet, in the Kenai Peninsula Borough about 110 miles (180 km) southwest of Anchorage. At 10,197 feet (3,108 m), in just over 5 miles (8.0 km) Mount Redoubt attains 9,150 feet (2,790 m) of prominence over its surrounding terrain. It is the highest summit in the Aleutian Range. In 1976, Redoubt Volcano was designated as a National Natural Landmark by the National Park Service.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Redoubt","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Rock_thin